china is conducting military maneuvers  in response to u.s house speaker nancy  pelosi's visit to taiwan  these are taking place in the skies and  waters around taiwan and are set to  expand to live fire drills from thursday  but they are being watched by countries  in the wider region foreign ministers of  southeast asian grouping of nations  asean are beginning a series of meetings  in cambodia to discuss current issues in  the region and it is already clear  tensions over taiwan  are set to figure in the talks  we hope that you know  concerned parties  do not take actions that may  deteriorate or escalate the tensions and  i don't think that the escalation of  tensions will  help them to solve the problems or you  know solve the uh differences  and i again uh emphasize the need you  know for all the concerned parties to  reaffirm you know the position to all of  these basic principles so that we can  make sure that you know taiwan street  will be a peaceful  part of this region and joining me now  from manila is richard hederia and he is  an author columnist and political  scientist and has written extensively  about china's relations with southeast  asian nations richard what do you see is  the wider impact of nancy pelosi's  taiwan visit on asean nations  well definitely there's the element of  geography right so if you're in the  philippines taiwan is just across the  luzon strait so if anything happens  there it will have a direct impact on  countries like the philippines there's  also the the aspect of south china sea  as you may know south china embraces  huge parts of taiwan and it's also where  a number of southeast asian countries  have claims and we have seen based on  recent reports that china  one of its retaliatory measures is to  conduct live fire  exercises across south china sea  following the visit of nancy pelosi uh  to taiwan the other thing of course is  the fact that taiwan and china both have  very good economic relationship with  many countries in the region so even  though majority of southeast asian  countries have a one china policy  meaning diplomatically they only  recognize beijing as a true  representative of greater china but they  have also very robust economic  investment ties with taiwan so that's  why this is very complicated for a lot  of us in the region  which is why asean nations have  generally been reluctant haven't they to  pick sides between the us and china when  it comes to their rivalry in the pacific  but do you think they are slowly being  pushed into the direction of picking one  well not picking a side is a picking  is a choice in itself right so  definitely there are countries in  southeast asia which will be  fence sitters uh forever they'll try to  do that but at the same time let's not  forget the philippines thailand are u.s  treaty allies so in an event of conflict  if god forbid it reaches that point you  know bases in the philippines in  thailand potentially could be used by  american forces by japanese forces by  australian forces in an event of  contingency and then you have other  countries like singapore for instance or  even indonesia which are  non-aligned in terms of their formal  diplomatic predisposition but especially  in the case of singapore it has emerged  as a vital vital de facto ally of the  united states so i mean the joke is that  you know countries like the philippines  are allies that don't act like allies  while you know countries like singapore  are not allies but they act like allies  right so this is also the situation we  have it's much more complicated than  people being prevaricating but it's more  also a question of new alignments and  dynamic realignments that are happening  in the region  given all of that would it would  southeast asian nations let's just say  asean would it have been uh better  placed if this visit  didn't go ahead would that have been the  preferred option for asean nations  well i'm sure even inside taiwan and as  in as much insight in washington dc we  have a lot of people have raised their  eyebrows and have been asking okay i  mean we appreciate the symbolism of this  but if china responds in an aggressive  way i mean who are we gonna fault here  right i mean you could say that maybe  the u.s was the one who provoked china  while others would say let's also see  what china will do because if china  shows restraint and so far they have  shown a degree of respect restraint that  that also says something about rising  china and what kind of superpower it's  going to be in the coming decades now as  far as southeast asian countries are  concerned i mean their hope is that  china does not respond aggressively and  doesn't deploy aircraft carriers among  others and that the us will not escalate  it because we don't want to be caught in  in between the giants as much as  possible including for u.s treaty allies  like the philippines  uh do you think that there is a risk in  the in the next few weeks at least that  beijing might uh become a more assertive  when it comes to the territorial  disputes with southeast asian nations  such as the philippines indonesia and so  on and so forth  well  if you look at a technical textual  analysis the words that president xi  jinping has used or the statements that  have come from chinese uh you know  state-owned uh newspapers they don't  indicate the kind of aggressive response  or military action we saw from china  let's say compared to 1995 1996 the last  time we had a huge crisis military  crisis in the taiwan strait at the same  time we know that president xi jinping  also is facing economic slowdown at home  he'll have and political transition  later this year so i think that timing  militates against a much more  militaristic response but a kind of a  mid-range uh sh you know a shot across  the bow kind of response i think that is  something we're gonna see but the  problem though is  mistakes can happen you can have  unintended uh clashes and incidents and  that's something we're very worried  about at the same time as you correctly  pointed out the other question is what  is china going to do to south asian  countries is it going to pressure  southeast asian countries to distance  themselves from nancy pelosi's visit or  even say something critical about it so  those are the things we're going to  watch out so a lot is really uh gonna  depend on what china gonna do on both  military and diplomatic front in the  coming months and weeks  richard always a pleasure talking to you  we'll have to leave it there thanks so  much for joining us today richard  hiderian  you
 

